  "summary": "Cyan shadows, hot skin: the blockbuster grade so common it became a meme.",
  "description": "The teal-and-orange grade is the digital-intermediate era's signature look: skin tones pushed warm-orange against shadows and skies crushed toward teal-cyan, exploiting maximal hue opposition while keeping faces luminous. Born when DI (digital intermediate) suites made per-channel grading trivial after O Brother, Where Art Thou? (2000) proved whole-film color control, the grade became blockbuster default — Transformers, Mad Max: Fury Road, every action key-art — and eventually a design meme (\"everything is orange and teal\"). Its power is real: complementary contrast plus protected skin reads well on dark screens. Its ubiquity made neutrality feel like rebellion.",
  "culturalContext": "The teal-and-orange grade is the digital era's first universal look: born in DI suites, it became blockbuster default and then design meme — its ubiquity made \"neutral grading\" feel rebellious, and every trailer and game key art still negotiates with its complementary logic.",
